ANANKE Mathematics Program — Prompt 11 of 30
Proof-Carrying Whole-Project Assurance, Evidence Dependency Calculus, Minimal Release Cores, Assumption Accounting, Incremental Recertification, and Independently Checkable Building Release
Abstract
Prompts 1–10 established:
[ \text{typed exact semantics} \longrightarrow \text{contextual quotients} \longrightarrow \text{decision width} \longrightarrow \text{symbolic quotient discovery} \longrightarrow \text{certified approximation} \longrightarrow \text{proof-carrying reduced physics} \longrightarrow \text{rewrite-aware reuse} \longrightarrow \text{causal executable macrostates} \longrightarrow \text{regret-closure metareasoning} \longrightarrow \text{adversarial claim breaking}. ]
Those mechanisms let ANANKE construct and defend individual claims. They do not by themselves establish that a complete building is ready for release.
Prompt 11 develops a typed Whole-Project Assurance Calculus.
An evidence object is not identified with the engineering claim it is intended to support. An Evidence Atom contains:
an exact artifact identity;
evidence kind;
the narrow evidentiary proposition directly established by the artifact;
authenticity and integrity state;
scope;
version;
authority;
validity interval;
applicability predicate;
uncertainty semantics;
provenance;
dependencies;
evidence status.
A genuine artifact may still be inapplicable. A cryptographically signed fire-test report can be authentic while applying to a different wall thickness, product revision, or fastener pattern. Therefore:
[ \boxed{ \text{authenticity} \neq \text{applicability} \neq \text{engineering truth}. } ]
Every assumption is first class. An Assumption Ledger records every premise on which a selected release derivation transitively depends, including:
model-validity assumptions;
occupancy limits;
load bounds;
construction tolerances;
code interpretations;
independence assumptions;
maintenance assumptions;
common-cause exclusions;
governed professional judgments.
A certified assurance inference is a typed semantic rule
[ r: (P_1,\ldots,P_k) \Longrightarrow C ]
whose soundness means:
[ \forall\omega\in\Omega_\vartheta, \qquad \bigwedge_{i=1}^{k} (\omega\models P_i) \Longrightarrow \omega\models C. ]
An assurance case is a typed AND/OR hypergraph. One inference route requires all of its premises; a claim may have several alternative inference routes. A valid derivation is a finite grounded DAG whose leaves are usable evidence, discharged assumptions, or lower-level certified claims and whose internal steps apply sound inference rules.
The central soundness theorem is:
[ \boxed{ \mathfrak H,K\vdash C \Longrightarrow \forall\omega\in\Gamma_{\mathrm{ass}}(\mathfrak H,K), \quad \omega\models C. } ]
This theorem requires:
grounded leaves;
sound rules;
active applicability;
matching fingerprints;
nonempty assurance foundations.
Circular metadata does not create proof. A cycle
[ C_1\Rightarrow C_2, \qquad C_2\Rightarrow C_1 ]
without an independent premise proves neither claim.
The complete release obligation manifest is
{R_1,\ldots,R_m}. ]
Whole-project assurance closure means that every manifest root has a checked derivation, every required assumption is discharged, every selected evidence object is usable, every required robustness and regret-closure claim is certified, every release version matches, and no blocking contradiction or unresolved obligation remains.
The final theorem is:
[ \boxed{ \forall R_i\in\mathfrak R,; \mathfrak H,K\vdash R_i \Longrightarrow \forall\omega\in\Gamma_{\mathrm{ass}}(\mathfrak H,K), \quad \bigwedge_{R_i\in\mathfrak R} (\omega\models R_i). } ]
This establishes release only relative to:
the declared manifest;
the declared theory;
the declared models;
the declared assumptions;
the declared evidence;
the declared authorities;
the declared adversarial domains.
It does not establish omitted obligations or unknown hazards.
Prompt 11 defines a Minimal Assurance Core. A set of external leaves (S) is a core for claim (C) when some valid derivation of (C) uses no external leaf outside (S). It is inclusion minimal when no proper subset remains sufficient.
Minimum-cost assurance-core extraction is NP-hard by reduction from Set Cover. A claim can also possess exponentially many distinct minimal cores.
The assurance dual is an Evidence Cut: a set of invalidatable leaves intersecting every valid derivation of the claim. The minimum cut cardinality
[ \lambda_{\mathrm{ass}}(C) ]
measures current assurance fragility.
The paper proves the exact duality:
[ H \text{ destroys every proof of }C \iff \forall S\in\mathsf{MAC}(C), \quad H\cap S\ne\varnothing. ]
Thus evidence cuts are hitting sets of the minimal-core hypergraph.
This is distinct from Prompt-10 physical robustness:
[ \rho_C^\star. ]
A building may be physically robust and evidentially fragile, or evidentially redundant and physically fragile.
Prompt 11 also develops dependency-directed incremental recertification. After a change (\Delta), ANANKE marks every directly changed root and every dependent proof artifact stale, but it does not invalidate a claim when an unaffected valid proof route survives. Under a complete dependency graph, complete changed-root identification, exact applicability rechecking, and deterministic local checking, incremental recertification returns the same release result as a full cold reconstruction.
The recertification frontier may stop at a claim node (q) when:
every path from the change to downstream release roots passes through (q);
the exact post-change report of (q) is unchanged;
its downstream-visible scope and semantics are unchanged.
Downstream semantic proof reconstruction is then unnecessary.
The final Proof-Carrying Release package contains only the selected release slice and the objects needed by an independent checker:
release manifest;
design and theory fingerprints;
assumptions;
selected derivations;
evidence atoms;
proof objects;
CAQC, CEDA, PCPE, CSSP, and adversarial certificates;
code interpretations;
professional judgments;
model-validity and discrepancy evidence;
consistency certificate;
provenance;
checker manifest;
unresolved blockers.
The architecture is:
[ \boxed{ \text{untrusted powerful generator} + \text{small trusted checker}. } ]
If the checker, inference rules, certificate kernels, applicability checks, and assumptions are sound, acceptance of the Proof-Carrying Release establishes every declared release obligation. The intelligence or reliability of the generator does not affect the meaning of an accepted proof.
The governing laws are:
[ \boxed{ \textbf{Every release claim must terminate in an explicit, typed, applicable, independently checkable chain of evidence and inference.} } ]
[ \boxed{ \textbf{When something changes, invalidate exactly what depends on it—no less and no more.} } ]
[ \boxed{ \textbf{Make the generator powerful. Make the checker small. Trust the proof, not the intelligence that proposed it.} } ]



1. Prompt-10 Proof-Registry Audit
The complete Prompt-10 paper is present in the active record. The following audit uses its body statements and proofs rather than its registry labels alone.
￼
No Prompt-10 theorem required by Prompt 11 exists only in its registry.
No Prompt-10 Completion Addendum is required.
Two scope conditions remain authoritative:
A robustness certificate supports only the adversarial domain named in its claim.
An evidence breaker can invalidate release support without proving physical failure.



2. Release Claims and Typed Release Semantics
Let:
[ \mathsf{Claim}_\vartheta ]
be the Prompt-10 claim universe for theory fingerprint (\vartheta).
Every claim (C) has:
exact report function [ r_C:\Omega_\vartheta\to\mathcal R_C; ]
accepted report set [ \mathcal G_C\subseteq\mathcal R_C. ]
Exact truth remains:
[ \omega\models C \iff r_C(\omega)\in\mathcal G_C. ]
Prompt 11 does not replace this semantics.
A release obligation may accept a qualified report. For example:
{ \mathsf{COMPLIANT}, \mathsf{COMPLIANT_WITH_AUTHORIZED_WAIVER}, \mathsf{NONCOMPLIANT}, \mathsf{UNRESOLVED} }. ]
The release policy explicitly declares which reports are accepted.
No scalar confidence score replaces these reports.



3. Evidence Atoms
DEF-ASS1 — Evidence Atom
An Evidence Atom is:
\left( \mathsf{id}_e, \mathsf{kind}_e, \mathsf{payload}_e, Q_e, \mathsf{scope}_e, \mathsf{issuer}_e, \mathsf{authority}_e, \mathsf{version}_e, \mathsf{validity}_e, \mathsf{auth}_e, \mathsf{app}_e, \mathsf{uncertainty}_e, \mathsf{provenance}_e, \mathsf{dependencies}_e, \mathsf{status}_e \right). ]
Where:
(\mathsf{id}_e) is persistent;
(\mathsf{kind}_e) is its evidence kind;
(\mathsf{payload}_e) is the raw proof, report, measurement, or certificate;
(Q_e) is the narrow typed evidentiary claim directly established by the artifact;
(\mathsf{scope}_e) identifies the product, subsystem, phase, jurisdiction, and use;
(\mathsf{issuer}_e) identifies origin;
(\mathsf{authority}_e) identifies authorized claim types;
(\mathsf{version}_e) records artifact and dependency versions;
(\mathsf{validity}_e) records time and parameter validity;
(\mathsf{auth}_e) records authenticity and integrity;
(\mathsf{app}_e) is the applicability predicate;
(\mathsf{uncertainty}_e) contains typed uncertainty semantics;
(\mathsf{provenance}_e) is the provenance chain;
(\mathsf{dependencies}_e) names required artifacts and assumptions;
(\mathsf{status}_e) is the current evidence status.
The evidentiary claim (Q_e) is deliberately narrow.
A laboratory report may directly establish:
Specimen S under protocol P achieved 120 minutes.
It does not directly establish:
Every wall using a visually similar product is a two-hour wall.
The latter requires additional identity, applicability, installation, and authority premises.



3.1 Evidence kinds
At minimum:
[ \begin{aligned} \mathsf{EvKind}={& \mathsf{FormalProof},\ &\mathsf{ProofAssistantTheorem},\ &\mathsf{SATCertificate},\ &\mathsf{SMTCertificate},\ &\mathsf{NumericalEnclosure},\ &\mathsf{FEMCertificate},\ &\mathsf{CFDCertificate},\ &\mathsf{CAQC},\ &\mathsf{CEDA},\ &\mathsf{PCPE},\ &\mathsf{CSSP},\ &\mathsf{ClaimBreaker},\ &\mathsf{RobustnessCoverage},\ &\mathsf{Measurement},\ &\mathsf{LaboratoryTest},\ &\mathsf{ManufacturerCertification},\ &\mathsf{FieldInspection},\ &\mathsf{CalibrationEvidence},\ &\mathsf{ModelValidation},\ &\mathsf{ModelDiscrepancyEvidence},\ &\mathsf{CodeInterpretation},\ &\mathsf{StandardInterpretation},\ &\mathsf{ProfessionalJudgment},\ &\mathsf{StakeholderApproval},\ &\mathsf{AssumptionDeclaration},\ &\mathsf{UnresolvedObligation} }. \end{aligned} ]
Different kinds have different admission rules.
There is no universal coercion:
[ \mathsf{ProfessionalJudgment} \to \mathsf{FormalProof}. ]



4. Evidence Status and Applicability
DEF-ASS2 — Evidence Status
{ \mathsf{VALID}, \mathsf{INVALID}, \mathsf{STALE}, \mathsf{OUTSIDE_DOMAIN}, \mathsf{CONTRADICTED}, \mathsf{UNRESOLVED} }. ]
Interpretations:
(\mathsf{VALID}): the artifact and its direct evidentiary claim pass their required checks;
(\mathsf{INVALID}): a required check failed;
(\mathsf{STALE}): a dependency or version changed;
(\mathsf{OUTSIDE_DOMAIN}): authentic evidence does not cover the current query;
(\mathsf{CONTRADICTED}): incompatible active evidence exists;
(\mathsf{UNRESOLVED}): validity has not been established.
No inference rule maps:
[ \mathsf{UNRESOLVED} ]
to:
[ \mathsf{VALID}. ]



DEF-ASS3 — Evidence Applicability
For evidence atom (e), claim (C), and exact database (\omega):
[ \mathsf{App}(e,C,\omega) ]
means that:
product identity matches;
geometry and assembly configuration match;
parameter values are inside the certified domain;
code edition and jurisdiction match;
lifecycle phase matches;
authority and scope match;
model and calibration versions match;
required dependencies remain valid.
For release over (\Gamma(K)), an unconditional evidence leaf requires:
[ \forall\omega\in\Gamma(K), \qquad \mathsf{App}(e,C,\omega). ]
If applicability varies across (\Gamma(K)), the assurance case must:
split the database family;
narrow (\Gamma(K));
add conditional derivations;
or remain unresolved.



4.1 Usable evidence
Define:
[ \mathsf{Usable}(e,C,K) ]
when:
(\mathsf{auth}_e) is accepted;
(\mathsf{status}_e=\mathsf{VALID});
every dependency is active;
versions match;
applicability holds throughout the claim’s exact project family;
the kind-specific checker accepts the artifact.
Usability does not by itself mean that (e) proves (C).
It means (e) may be used as a premise in a sound inference route.



5. Authentication Does Not Prove Engineering Truth
THM-ASS1 — Authentication Does Not Establish Technical Truth
There is no valid general implication:
[ \mathsf{Authentic}(e) \Longrightarrow \omega\models C. ]
Proof
Let (e) be a perfectly signed laboratory report for product revision (P_1).
Let the installed project database (\omega) contain product revision (P_2), and suppose:
[ P_1\ne P_2. ]
The cryptographic signature correctly establishes:
artifact origin;
artifact integrity;
issuer identity.
The artifact is authentic.
Let claim (C) be:
The installed product (P_2) has the tested rating.
The signed report contains no statement about (P_2), so (C) may be false.
Therefore authenticity does not entail technical truth.
The missing requirement is applicability:
[ \mathsf{App}(e,C,\omega). ]
∎
Thus:
[ \boxed{ \text{cryptographic validity} \neq \text{engineering validity}. } ]



6. Assurance Assumptions
DEF-ASS4 — Assurance Assumption
An Assurance Assumption is:
\left( \mathsf{id}_a, P_a, \mathsf{scope}_a, \mathsf{source}_a, \mathsf{mode}_a, \mathsf{status}_a, \mathsf{evidence}_a, \mathsf{dependencies}_a \right), ]
where:
(P_a:\Omega_\vartheta\to\mathsf{Prop}) is the exact proposition;
(\mathsf{scope}_a) identifies subsystem, phase, and validity;
(\mathsf{source}_a) identifies origin;
(\mathsf{mode}_a) may be:
formally proved;
empirically supported;
contractually imposed;
governed judgment;
modeling premise;
unresolved;
(\mathsf{status}_a) records whether it is discharged;
(\mathsf{evidence}_a) names supporting evidence;
(\mathsf{dependencies}_a) names lower assumptions.
Examples include:
occupancy bound;
material-strength range;
support boundary condition;
maintenance frequency;
construction tolerance;
independence assumption;
common-cause exclusion;
model-validity assumption;
code interpretation;
professional judgment.



DEF-ASS5 — Assumption Ledger
The Assumption Ledger is:
[ \mathcal L_A. ]
It contains every assumption appearing in the transitive dependency closure of any selected release derivation.
The ledger records:
assumption proposition;
status;
source;
support;
scope;
version;
affected claims;
defeaters.
A release derivation is ledger complete only when every assumption leaf occurs in (\mathcal L_A).



7. Missing Premises
THM-ASS2 — Missing Necessary Premise Invalidates Assurance
Suppose the inference:
[ P_1\land\cdots\land P_k \Longrightarrow C ]
is sound.
Suppose further that:
[ P_1\land\cdots\land P_{k-1} \not\models C. ]
Then an assurance record containing only:
[ P_1,\ldots,P_{k-1} ]
does not establish (C).
Proof
Because:
[ P_1\land\cdots\land P_{k-1} \not\models C, ]
there exists an exact database (\omega_0) satisfying every recorded premise while violating (C):
[ \omega_0\models P_i \quad (i<k), ]
[ \omega_0\not\models C. ]
Therefore the recorded premise set is consistent with a world in which the conclusion is false.
It does not entail (C).
The omitted premise (P_k) may be the distinction between (\omega_0) and a world in which the complete inference succeeds. ∎
The premise must be logically necessary for this result. Redundant premises may be omitted after a proof of redundancy.



7.1 Two-world engineering example
Recorded premise:
[ P_1= \text{the nonlinear solver residual is below tolerance}. ]
Omitted premise:
[ P_2= \text{the model is valid for the current buckling regime}. ]
Conclusion:
[ C= \text{the physical member is safe}. ]
Two worlds may share (P_1) while differing on (P_2) and (C).
A converged solver does not discharge model validity.



8. Certified Assurance Inference
DEF-ASS6 — Certified Assurance Inference
A typed inference rule is:
[ r: (P_1,\ldots,P_k) \Longrightarrow C. ]
It contains:
rule ID;
typed premise list;
conclusion;
theory fingerprint;
scope;
side conditions represented as explicit premises;
proof of soundness;
checker identity.
The rule is sound when:
[ \boxed{ \forall\omega\in\Omega_\vartheta, \qquad \bigwedge_{i=1}^{k} (\omega\models P_i) \Longrightarrow \omega\models C. } ]
Evidence-to-claim inference is represented by such a rule.
Evidence does not bypass inference.



8.1 Example: fire-test admission rule
Premises:
[ \begin{aligned} P_1&=\text{test report authentically establishes specimen result},\ P_2&=\text{installed assembly identity matches specimen},\ P_3&=\text{product revision matches},\ P_4&=\text{fastener spacing matches},\ P_5&=\text{jurisdiction accepts the test basis}. \end{aligned} ]
Conclusion:
[ C= \text{installed assembly has the required evidence classification}. ]
The test report alone establishes only (P_1).



9. Assurance Case Hypergraph
To avoid collision with Prompt-8 action notation (\mathcal A), denote the assurance case by:
[ \mathfrak H. ]
DEF-ASS7 — Assurance Case
\left( V_C, V_E, V_A, V_R, H, \vartheta \right), ]
where:
(V_C): claim nodes;
(V_E): evidence atoms;
(V_A): assumptions;
(V_R): inference rules;
(H): typed inference hyperedges;
(\vartheta): theory fingerprint.
A hyperedge has form:
[ h: {p_1,\ldots,p_k} \xrightarrow{r} C. ]
Within one hyperedge the premises are conjunctive.
Several incoming hyperedges to the same claim represent alternative proof routes.
Thus the assurance case is naturally AND/OR structured.



10. Release Manifest
DEF-ASS8 — Release Obligation Manifest
{ R_1,\ldots,R_m } \subseteq V_C ]
is the complete declared release-obligation manifest.
Each root records:
exact claim;
accepted reports;
phase;
authority;
jurisdiction;
required evidence type;
required robustness domain;
required decision tolerance.
Possible sources include:
code;
contract;
owner requirements;
professional governance;
internal release policy;
protected architectural intent.
Release completeness is always relative to (\mathfrak R).



THM-ASS3 — Assurance Completeness Is Relative to the Obligation Manifest
Proving every:
[ R_i\in\mathfrak R ]
does not establish an omitted obligation:
[ R^\star\notin\mathfrak R ]
unless a separate theorem establishes that the manifest is exhaustive.
Proof
Construct an exact database (\omega) satisfying every listed root:
[ \omega\models R_i \qquad \forall R_i\in\mathfrak R, ]
while:
[ \omega\not\models R^\star. ]
The conjunction of listed roots remains true.
Therefore it does not entail the omitted obligation. ∎
This is the release analogue of Prompt-10’s open-world hazard limitation.



11. Assurance Derivations
DEF-ASS9 — Assurance Derivation
A derivation (D) of claim (C) is a finite rooted acyclic DAG satisfying:
the root is (C);
every internal claim node selects one incoming sound inference hyperedge;
every premise of that hyperedge is derived by a predecessor node;
every leaf is one of:
usable evidence;
discharged assumption;
accepted trusted axiom;
independently certified lower-level claim;
every version and applicability check passes;
every referenced defeater is inactive.
Shared subproofs may appear once and feed multiple conclusions.



DEF-ASS10 — Assurance-Closed Claim
Notation:
[ \mathfrak H,K\vdash C ]
means that:
the assurance foundations are consistent;
a valid accepted derivation (D) of (C) exists in (\mathfrak H);
all leaves are valid throughout the relevant exact database family;
all rule and version checks pass.
The claim is then assurance closed.



12. Circular Support
A software dependency graph may contain cycles.
An ordinary proof derivation may not justify itself.
THM-ASS4 — Ungrounded Circular Support Proves Nothing
Let:
[ C_1\Rightarrow C_2, ]
[ C_2\Rightarrow C_3, ]
[ \cdots ]
[ C_n\Rightarrow C_1 ]
be the only available premises.
No (C_i) is thereby established.
Proof
Consider an exact database (\omega) in which:
[ \omega\not\models C_i \qquad \forall i. ]
Every implication remains true, because each implication has a false antecedent.
Thus all inference-rule propositions can hold while every (C_i) is false.
No (C_i) follows from the cycle alone. ∎
A legitimate coinductive proof requires a separately sound coinduction rule. It is not ordinary circular support.



13. Assurance Foundation Consistency
DEF-ASS31 — Assurance Consistency
Let:
[ \mathsf{Found}(\mathfrak H,K) ]
be the set of active foundational exact propositions supplied by:
assumptions;
direct evidentiary claims;
model-validity claims;
version constraints;
applicability constraints.
Define:
\left{ \omega\in\Gamma(K): \forall P\in\mathsf{Found}(\mathfrak H,K), ; \omega\models P \right}. ]
The assurance case is semantically consistent when:
[ \boxed{ \Gamma_{\mathrm{ass}}(\mathfrak H,K)\ne\varnothing. } ]



THM-ASS23 — Inconsistent Foundations Cannot Be Used as a Valid Release Proof
If:
[ \Gamma_{\mathrm{ass}}(\mathfrak H,K)=\varnothing, ]
the release checker must reject complete release.
Proof
In classical semantics:
[ \forall\omega\in\varnothing, \quad \omega\models C ]
is vacuously true for every (C).
Allowing an empty exact-database family would therefore permit an inconsistent premise set to “prove” arbitrary release claims.
The release policy adds the explicit nonemptiness requirement.
An inconsistent case fails that requirement and cannot release. ∎



14. Derivation Soundness
THM-ASS5 — Assurance Derivation Soundness
Assume:
(\Gamma_{\mathrm{ass}}(\mathfrak H,K)\ne\varnothing);
every evidence leaf establishes its direct evidentiary claim throughout (\Gamma_{\mathrm{ass}});
every assumption leaf is true throughout (\Gamma_{\mathrm{ass}});
every applicability predicate holds;
every inference rule is sound;
every version and dependency check passes.
Then:
[ \boxed{ \mathfrak H,K\vdash C \Longrightarrow \forall\omega\in\Gamma_{\mathrm{ass}}(\mathfrak H,K), \quad \omega\models C. } ]
Proof
Topologically order the finite derivation DAG.
For each leaf:
evidence-leaf truth follows from assumptions 2 and 4;
assumption-leaf truth follows from assumption 3;
lower-level certified claim truth follows from its checked certificate.
Assume inductively that every predecessor premise of an internal rule node is true in arbitrary:
[ \omega\in\Gamma_{\mathrm{ass}}. ]
Rule soundness gives truth of the rule’s conclusion in (\omega).
Proceeding in topological order establishes every derivation node, including the root (C).
Because (\omega) was arbitrary, (C) holds throughout (\Gamma_{\mathrm{ass}}). ∎
This is the foundational assurance theorem.



15. Alternative Proof Routes
A claim may have derivations:
[ D_1, \qquad D_2. ]
Invalidating one route does not invalidate the claim when another route remains valid.
The assurance engine therefore maintains separately:
proof-artifact validity;
derivation validity;
claim support status;
exact claim truth.
A claim is currently supported when at least one valid derivation survives.



16. Support Sets and Minimal Assurance Cores
DEF-ASS11 — Support Set
For derivation (D):
[ \mathsf{Leaves}(D) ]
is the set of externally supplied, invalidatable evidence and assumption leaves used by (D).
Trusted inference rules and checker kernels are recorded separately in the TCB.



DEF-ASS12 — Minimal Assurance Core
A finite leaf set (S) supports claim (C) when there exists a valid derivation (D) such that:
[ \mathsf{Leaves}(D)\subseteq S. ]
It is an Assurance Core when it supports (C).
It is inclusion minimal when:
[ S\text{ supports }C, ]
and:
[ \forall S'\subsetneq S, \qquad S'\text{ does not support }C. ]
Define:
[ \mathsf{MAC}(C) ]
as the family of all inclusion-minimal assurance cores.
For release roots (\mathfrak R), define (\mathsf{MAC}(\mathfrak R)) analogously using derivations for every root.



16.1 Minimum-cost core
Given nonnegative leaf costs:
[ w:\mathcal L_{\mathrm{ext}}\to[0,\infty), ]
define:
\sum_{e\in S}w(e). ]
A minimum-cost core is:
[ S^\star \in \arg\min_{S\text{ supports }C} \mathsf{CoreCost}(S). ]
Cost may represent:
audit time;
storage;
checker effort;
external review cost.
It is not automatically evidence strength.



17. Minimum Assurance Core Hardness
THM-ASS6 — Minimum Assurance Core Is NP-Hard
Computing a minimum-cardinality or minimum-cost Assurance Core is NP-hard.
Proof by reduction from Set Cover
Take a Set Cover instance:
[ U={u_1,\ldots,u_n}, ]
[ \mathcal S={S_1,\ldots,S_m}. ]
Create one release obligation claim:
[ O_u ]
for every (u\in U).
Create root:
\bigwedge_{u\in U}O_u. ]
For every set (S_j), create evidence atom (e_j).
For every:
[ u\in S_j, ]
add sound rule:
[ e_j\Longrightarrow O_u. ]
Finally add the conjunction rule:
[ \bigwedge_{u\in U}O_u \Longrightarrow C_{\mathrm{release}}. ]
A selected evidence set (E) supports (C_{\mathrm{release}}) exactly when, for every (u), at least one selected (e_j) satisfies:
[ u\in S_j. ]
Thus selected evidence sets supporting release correspond exactly to set covers.
Minimum evidence-core size equals minimum set-cover size.
The weighted version follows by assigning evidence cost equal to set cost. ∎



PRO-ASS1 — Exponentially Many Minimal Assurance Cores
There exist assurance cases with:
[ 2^n ]
distinct minimal cores.
Construction
Create obligations:
[ O_1,\ldots,O_n. ]
For each (i), create two alternative evidence atoms:
[ e_i^0, \qquad e_i^1, ]
each of which individually establishes (O_i).
Release requires:
[ \bigwedge_iO_i. ]
Every minimal core chooses exactly one evidence atom from each pair.
The number of choices is:
[ 2^n. ]
∎
An independent checker need not enumerate every valid proof route.



18. Release Slices
DEF-ASS13 — Release Slice
Choose one valid active derivation (D_i) for every release root:
[ R_i\in\mathfrak R. ]
The selected Release Slice is the union of:
all nodes in (D_i);
all dependency closures required to check those nodes;
all referenced TCB rule identities;
all version and applicability records.
Denote it:
[ \mathsf{Slice}(D_1,\ldots,D_m). ]



THM-ASS7 — Selected Derivation Slice Sufficiency
If every release root has one valid checked derivation entirely contained in slice (S), then artifacts outside (S) are unnecessary for verifying those selected derivations under the fixed theory.
Proof
Verification of a derivation uses only:
its nodes;
its leaves;
its inference rules;
their dependencies;
the TCB.
By construction, all such objects lie in (S).
No checker query for the selected derivations references an artifact outside (S).
Therefore removing outside artifacts does not change verification of the selected derivations. ∎
Outside artifacts may still have:
future value;
alternative-proof value;
recertification value;
audit value.



19. Evidence Cuts and Assurance Redundancy
DEF-ASS14 — Evidence Cut
Let:
[ \mathcal D_C ]
be the family of currently valid derivations of claim (C).
An Evidence Cut is a set (H) of invalidatable external leaves such that:
[ \forall D\in\mathcal D_C, \qquad H\cap\mathsf{Leaves}(D)\ne\varnothing. ]
Invalidating every leaf in (H) destroys every current derivation of (C).



DEF-ASS15 — Assurance Redundancy
\min \left{ |H|: H\text{ is an Evidence Cut for }C \right}. } ]
