51. Assurance Blocker Frontiers
DEF-ASS27 — Assurance Blocker Frontier
For an unsupported release root (R), let:
[ \mathcal D_R^{\mathrm{cand}} ]
be the current candidate derivation family.
For each candidate derivation (D), let:
[ U(D) ]
be its unresolved or invalid external leaves.
A Blocker Frontier is an inclusion-minimal set (B) satisfying:
[ B\cap U(D)\ne\varnothing \qquad \forall D\in\mathcal D_R^{\mathrm{cand}}. ]
For several unsupported roots, the condition applies to every candidate release derivation forest.
A blocker frontier says:
Every currently available release route is obstructed by at least one blocker in this set.
Resolving every member of a blocker frontier need not be the cheapest release strategy.
Blocker explanation and blocker-resolution planning are different problems.



52. Minimum Blocker-Resolution Hardness
THM-ASS25 — Minimum Blocker-Resolution Selection Is NP-Hard
Finding the minimum-cost set of evidence acquisitions or computations sufficient to close all release obligations is NP-hard.
Proof by reduction from Set Cover
Take Set Cover instance:
[ U={u_1,\ldots,u_n}, \qquad \mathcal S={S_1,\ldots,S_m}. ]
Create one unresolved release obligation (O_u) for each (u).
Create one computation (c_j) for each (S_j).
A successful execution of (c_j) discharges exactly obligations:
[ {O_u\in S_j}. ]
Assume deterministic success and no other proof routes.
A computation set closes every release obligation exactly when the corresponding subsets cover (U).
Minimum-cost blocker resolution therefore equals minimum-cost Set Cover. ∎
Prompt 9 remains the scheduler.
Prompt 11 exposes blockers and evidence-acquisition actions to it.



