112. What Has Now Been Proven
Evidence semantics
Evidence is a typed first-class object.
Authenticity, technical validity, applicability, and claim truth are distinct.
Evidence statuses preserve invalid, stale, outside-domain, contradicted, and unresolved states.
A signed artifact does not establish an inapplicable engineering claim.
Each evidence kind requires its own sound admission rule.
Assumption accounting
Every assurance assumption is an explicit proposition.
A complete selected release argument has an Assumption Ledger.
Omitting a nonredundant premise leaves the conclusion unproved.
Formal proofs establish implications relative to assumptions, not the assumptions themselves.
Assurance inference
A certified inference rule has exact semantic soundness.
Assurance cases are typed AND/OR hypergraphs.
Multiple proof routes are distinct from claim truth.
Ungrounded circular support proves nothing.
A valid grounded derivation establishes its root throughout the exact assurance database family.
Whole-project derivation
Release roots are defined by a typed manifest.
Manifest completeness is relative, not absolute.
Selected valid derivations suffice to establish every listed root.
Whole-project assurance closure is distinct from individual certificate validity.
Contradictions and defeaters
Contradictory active exact reports block unique release classification.
Defeaters invalidate every dependent proof path.
Alternative undefeated proof paths may remain valid.
New evidence is not monotone when it establishes a defeater or inconsistency.
Minimal release cores
Assurance Cores identify sufficient external support sets.
Inclusion minimality, minimum cardinality, and minimum cost are different.
Minimum-core extraction is NP-hard.
A finite case may have exponentially many minimal cores.
One selected release slice is sufficient to verify its selected derivations.
Assurance redundancy
Assurance redundancy is the minimum number of evidence leaves whose invalidation destroys every current proof route.
Many proof paths can still have redundancy one.
Assurance redundancy and physical robustness are independent.
A small release core may be evidentially fragile.
Evidence-cut duality
Evidence Cuts are exactly hitting sets of the minimal-core hypergraph.
Minimum Evidence Cut is NP-hard.
Evidence cuts are the assurance analogue of Prompt-10 evidence breakers.
Model and numerical evidence
A numerical enclosure is a model-level claim.
A physical claim additionally requires model validity and discrepancy evidence.
PCPE applicability conditions are explicit proof premises.
Solver convergence alone is not whole-project assurance.
Adversarial evidence
Robustness certificates support only their declared adversarial domain.
Claim Breakers support only their checked scope.
Evidence invalidation and physical failure remain separate.
Prompt-10 red teaming can attack release derivations.
Professional judgment
Professional judgment is scoped by authority, claim type, jurisdiction, phase, and conditions.
A human signature does not become universal physical truth.
Human judgment can discharge only governance-authorized claim types.
Code interpretation
Code interpretations are edition and jurisdiction scoped.
Old-code compliance does not imply new-code compliance.
Raw geometry may remain valid while compliance inference changes.
Versioning
Every release package is bound to design, model, evidence, code, solver, theory, and checker versions.
A PCR for one design hash does not prove another design.
Historical releases remain auditable rather than being silently overwritten.
Incremental recertification
Changed roots and descendants can be invalidated precisely.
Alternative surviving proof routes preserve claim support.
Under complete dependencies and complete local rechecking, incremental recertification equals cold reconstruction.
Recertification may stop at an unchanged sufficient assurance frontier.
Theory restriction can permit proof reuse.
Theory expansion does not automatically inherit the old proof.
Repeated-motif assurance
A checked parametric proof template may be instantiated without repeating proof search.
Template validity domains and exceptions remain explicit.
Repeated proof sharing reduces logical and storage duplication.
Named exceptions are not forced into the template.
Proof checking
Explicit proof-DAG checking is linear in graph size plus local certificate-check costs.
Proof discovery may remain NP-hard while checking a supplied proof is inexpensive.
A short proof is not guaranteed to exist.
Checker incompleteness is compatible with soundness.
Trusted computing base
Specialized sound checkers compose through sound assurance rules.
The generator may remain outside the release TCB.
A malicious or incompetent generator cannot make an invalid PCR pass a sound checker.
Cryptographic checking protects integrity, not engineering meaning.
Whole-project release
Assurance consistency is mandatory.
Inconsistent foundations cannot vacuously prove release.
Every release root must have one valid grounded derivation.
Whole-project Proof-Carrying Release establishes all declared manifest roots throughout the exact assurance database family.
The theorem is relative to the declared theory, models, assumptions, evidence, adversarial domains, authorities, phase, and manifest.
It does not establish omitted obligations or unknown hazards.
RCVOC integration
Assurance blockers can be exported as Prompt-9 computation actions.
Minimum blocker-resolution planning is NP-hard.
Prompt 9 remains responsible for choosing what assurance computation to run next.
One common evidence dependency used by many release claims may have very high RCVOC.
The central assurance conclusion is:
[ \boxed{ \begin{minipage}{0.88\linewidth} A whole building is releaseable only when every declared release obligation has at least one grounded, typed, applicable, version-compatible, independently checked derivation from consistent assumptions and valid evidence. \end{minipage} } ]
The central trust conclusion is:
[ \boxed{ \textbf{The generator may be enormous. The release argument can be compact. Correctness belongs to the checker.} } ]



