97. What Has Now Been Proven
Architectural intent semantics
Sovereign Design States contain physical, architectural, authority, provenance, consent, and evidence state.
Architectural intent is represented as a typed executable program rather than a label or scalar score.
Intent rules are scope and version aware.
Geometrically identical design states may differ sovereignly.
Hard constraints and vetoes
Hard intent predicates define an admissible set.
Noncompensatory vetoes exclude candidates regardless of lower-tier gains.
No finite penalty universally represents a hard constraint when compensating objectives are unbounded.
A finite penalty can encode a hard restriction on a known bounded domain, but without sovereignty semantics.
Optimizing over the sovereignty-feasible set preserves every hard invariant by construction.
Partial and incomplete preference
Architectural preference need not be complete.
Incomparability is an explicit report.
A genuinely incomplete relation cannot be exactly represented by ordinary one-dimensional real utility.
A heuristic utility may be used only with declared authority and scope.
Lexicographic and noncompensatory priority
Sequential constrained optimization exactly implements finite lexicographic optimization under attainment assumptions.
No fixed finite weight ratio universally reproduces lexicographic priority over unbounded lower-tier variation.
On a bounded positively separated domain, a sufficiently large finite weight ratio reproduces the ordering.
Noncompensatory commitments are represented directly rather than through accidental weight scales.
Human option preservation
The Human Option Set contains designs selectable under at least one currently admitted human choice theory.
A selectable option without an equivalent representative cannot be removed by an option-preserving optimizer.
Preference refinement shrinks the option set under a fixed candidate universe.
Design search can enlarge the option set.
Human preference-query selection is NP-hard even in a restricted static model.
Menu-sensitive architectural choice
Choice correspondences may depend on the full menu.
Pairwise preference or ranking does not determine all sovereign reports.
A candidate may affect selection even when it is not itself selected.
Pruning requires complete decision irrelevance.
Design authority
Proposal, execution, approval, veto, and delegation are distinct rights.
Proposal authority does not imply execution authority.
Authority is action and scope specific.
Consent
Consent artifacts are action, scope, version, condition, and time specific.
Consent-required actions may be proposed but not committed without valid consent.
A consent artifact cannot authorize an unrelated action or version.
Trace-sensitive sovereignty
Sovereignty can depend on the transformation trace.
Identical final geometry need not imply identical sovereign status.
A geometry-only metric cannot detect every procedural sovereignty violation.
Sequentially composing preserving actions preserves the contract under the stated predecessor-domain assumptions.
Authorship provenance
Authorship provenance is a governed project object.
Authorship-preserving transformations are relative to an explicit policy.
Final-shape similarity does not establish authorship preservation.
Noninterference
Protected-Intent Influence Graphs identify possible paths to protected meaning.
Per-step noninterference composes over finite action sequences.
A certified noninterfering action language may be optimized without changing protected endpoint observations.
Discipline names do not prove noninterference.
Conflict detection
Sovereignty feasibility is exact intersection of engineering and intent feasibility.
Empty intersection produces an intent conflict, not an automatic relaxation.
Minimal Intent Conflict Cores identify jointly impossible commitment subsets.
A conflict core does not choose which commitment should be abandoned.
Intent relaxation
