48. Materialization Hardness
THM-MAT30 — Restricted Finite Materialization Realizability Is NP-Complete
Consider the restricted materialization problem in which:
there is one semantic state (q_\varphi);
a concrete artifact is a Boolean vector [ b\in{0,1}^n; ]
the materialization relation is [ q_\varphi\mathrel{\mathcal R_{\mathrm{mat}}}b \iff \varphi(b) ] for an input 3-CNF formula (\varphi).
Then deciding
[ \mathsf{Realizable}(q_\varphi) ]
is NP-complete.
Proof
Membership in NP
A certificate is a Boolean vector (b).
Evaluating a 3-CNF formula on (b) takes polynomial time.
Thus the problem is in NP.
NP-hardness
Given any 3-SAT instance (\varphi), construct (q_\varphi) and the relation above.
Then:
[ \mathsf{Realizable}(q_\varphi) ]
holds exactly when there exists (b) satisfying (\varphi).
Therefore 3-SAT reduces to restricted materialization realizability.
The problem is NP-hard.
Combined with membership in NP, it is NP-complete. ∎



THM-MAT31 — Minimum-Cost Product-Family Materialization Is NP-Hard
Consider a restricted product-materialization problem.
Let requirement universe be
[ U={u_1,\ldots,u_m}. ]
Each available product family (p_j) satisfies requirement subset
[ S_j\subseteq U. ]
A product-family selection is valid when its union covers every requirement:
[ \bigcup_{p_j\in P'}S_j=U. ]
The objective minimizes total product-family cost.
Then minimum-cost product-family materialization is NP-hard.
Proof by reduction from Weighted Set Cover
Take an arbitrary Weighted Set Cover instance:
[ U, \quad {S_1,\ldots,S_n}, \quad w_1,\ldots,w_n. ]
Create one product family (p_j) for each set (S_j), with cost (w_j).
Define materialization validity exactly as full requirement coverage.
Then every valid product-family assignment is a set cover, and every set cover is a valid assignment.
Costs agree.
Thus an optimal product-family materialization solves Weighted Set Cover.
Weighted Set Cover is NP-hard. ∎
Real product configuration can additionally contain:
pairwise incompatibilities;
geometric coupling;
connector choices;
evidence restrictions;
quantity constraints.
The restricted hardness theorem does not capture all of that additional difficulty.



49. Checking Complexity
PRO-MAT3 — Explicit PCMP DAG Checking Bound
Suppose a PCMP checker processes an acyclic dependency graph with:
(N) nodes;
(E) dependency edges;
local checker costs (c_1,\ldots,c_N).
Assume each node is checked once after its dependencies.
Then total checking time is
[ O \left( N+E+\sum_{i=1}^{N}c_i \right). ]
Proof
A topological traversal examines every node once and every edge once, contributing (O(N+E)).
The local checker at node (i) contributes (c_i).
Summing gives the bound. ∎
Checking can be much cheaper than witness discovery.
It is not automatically cheap if:
a local geometry certificate is enormous;
the proof package is huge;
a checker reruns an expensive full solve;
the graph is not normalized.



