62. Negative Results Established by Prompt 13
Semantic success does not imply materialization success.
A canonical quotient class is inhabited, but a recombined abstract tuple may be spurious.
Set-theoretic section existence does not imply efficient witness extraction.
A semantic class may have no canonical symmetry-equivariant witness.
A semantic proof does not transport when its claim varies inside the materialization fiber.
Decision separators may be too coarse for exact gluing.
Hidden cross-boundary dependencies make local substitution unsound.
Nonempty adjacent refinement relations do not guarantee a coherent refinement chain.
Pairwise consistency can fail globally on cyclic covers.
Separator class IDs without witnesses or extraction oracles are not constructive.
An abstract topology need not have a geometric embedding.
Equal product capacity does not imply substitutability.
Continuous optima may not correspond to available products.
Nominal realizability does not imply robust realizability.
Scenario-specific realizability does not imply one fixed buildable design.
Clairvoyant scenario plans do not imply nonanticipative construction recourse.
Independent tolerance intervals can destroy valid correlation.
Static final-state feasibility does not imply constructibility.
A drawing or BIM representation is not automatically coherent with other project documents.
Round-trip geometry preservation does not imply evidence or sovereignty preservation.
A semantic lower bound need not be attained.
Heuristic materialization failure is not proof of unrealizability.
Arbitrary witness selection can destroy human architectural options.
Exact materialization is NP-hard in a restricted finite subclass.
Minimum-cost product-family configuration is NP-hard.
A checked design package is not the physical as-built building.



63. Falsification and Regression Suite
F1 — Inhabited canonical quotient
Every quotient class generated from an exact carrier must expose at least one source representative.
F2 — Spurious Cartesian abstraction
The states ((0,1)) and ((1,0)) in the MAT1 example must be rejected as concrete states.
F3 — Symmetry obstruction
A symmetric fiber with no fixed point must reject a claim of unique equivariant witness selection.
F4 — Fiber-invariant transport
A transported invariant must hold for every enumerated concrete witness in a finite benchmark.
F5 — Fiber-varying claim
The checker must require direct revalidation.
F6 — Refinement tower
A final artifact must project to every earlier level.
F7 — Nonsurjective refinement
A schematic state with no detailed lift must become PROVED_UNREALIZABLE only with a checked proof.
F8 — Tree gluing
Compatible local witnesses on a join tree must reconstruct globally.
F9 — Cyclic pairwise failure
Use:
[ x=y, \quad y=z, \quad x\neq z. ]
The checker must reject global realizability.
F10 — Missing interface variable
Omit one shared resource or connector and generate a false local solution.
The realization-completeness test must detect the defect.
F11 — DP backpointer extraction
Reconstruct the exact witness associated with an accepted root message.
F12 — Finite self-reduction
Recover a known finite witness using prefix-extension queries.
F13 — Exact topology
Introduce one connectivity mismatch and reject the artifact.
F14 — Numerical geometry boundary
An intersection enclosure crossing zero must remain unresolved.
F15 — Product mismatch
Products with equal capacity but incompatible connectors or evidence must fail substitution.
F16 — Catalog gap
A continuous optimum must not be labeled realized.
F17 — Quantifier order
Scenario-specific tolerance feasibility must not be labeled fixed robust.
F18 — Shared tolerance
One common translation must not be duplicated independently.
F19 — Static versus constructible
A clash-free final state without a valid sequence must not certify constructibility.
F20 — Temporary-state safety
A final safe structure with one unsafe erection state must fail the sequence certificate.
F21 — Document coherence
One stale schedule must invalidate the document-coherence claim.
F22 — Serialization scope
A geometry-only export must not claim evidence preservation.
F23 — Realizability cut
No cut may exclude a semantic state with a known checked witness.
F24 — UNKNOWN
An unresolved materializer result must not generate a cut or positive witness.
F25 — (\varepsilon)-optimality
Compare the certified bound against exhaustive finite concrete optimization.
F26 — Sovereignty fiber
A fiber containing architecturally distinct options must be refined or represented by multiple witnesses.
F27 — Incremental rematerialization
Incremental and cold checking must agree on finite benchmarks.
F28 — Materialization red team
Insert one deliberate product, tolerance, gluing, or sequence error and require a checked breaker.
F29 — PCMP independence
The checker receives the PCMP without optimization history and verifies it.
F30 — PCR integration
Prompt-11 release must remain blocked when a required materialization claim is unresolved.



