83. What Has Now Been Proven
Design versus physical reality
A checked design artifact and the physical building are different semantic objects.
A valid planned construction sequence proves that a legal path exists.
It does not prove that the physical project followed that path.
Design release and physical release are distinct.
Physical execution traces
Physical construction is modeled as a typed nondeterministic transition relation.
The actual execution trace may remain unknown.
A set of traces can be maintained instead of inventing one exact history.
Event evidence and capture completeness
Field event records are evidence about actions, not the actions themselves.
Event-capture completeness must be explicit.
Identical logs can correspond to different physical traces.
Absence from a log does not prove absence from the world.
Measurement semantics
A measurement is an exact relation between physical states and reported observations under a calibration state.
Raw bytes are not physical truth.
A point estimate is not the certification object unless exactness is proved.
Calibration
Calibration changes the measurement relation.
Authentic bytes can correspond to different physical intervals under different calibration states.
Measurement-based release requires applicable calibration.
Physical set-membership estimation
The authoritative physical estimate is a set of possible states.
Sound transition and observation updates preserve the actual state.
Repeated sound updates preserve actual-state containment through the entire execution trace.
Pure observation refinement cannot enlarge the physical knowledge set.
Evidence conflicts
An empty physical knowledge set means that accepted premises are inconsistent.
It does not mean the physical building does not exist.
Empty foundations block release.
As-built conformance
Physical conformance is a typed relation.
One geometric distance cannot generally encode all conformance semantics.
If the complete physical enclosure lies in the accepted set, actual conformance follows.
If the enclosure is disjoint from the accepted set, actual nonconformance follows.
Mixed enclosures remain unresolved.
Release without exact reconstruction
Exact physical-state reconstruction is unnecessary when every remaining state has one accepted release report.
Release concerns report certainty, not omniscience.
Field contextual equivalence
Prompt-2 contextual equivalence extends to physical field experiments.
Field-equivalent states are indistinguishable by the current complete experiment theory.
Physical identifiability
Observation-equivalent states with different release reports prove that the field theory is insufficient.
The correct response is richer evidence or unresolved release.
Claim-directed measurement
A claim-sufficient suite need not reconstruct the entire physical state.
It needs only to refine the relevant claim partition.
Minimum claim-sufficient experiment selection is NP-hard.
Correlated field uncertainty
Joint calibration, registration, and datum errors must remain joint.
Independent marginalization can change conformance decisions.
Shared error components can cancel in relative measurements.
Certified as-built geometry
A scan reconstruction must denote an outer physical geometry set.
Universal claims checked over the entire set hold for the actual geometry.
Occluded geometry cannot be silently copied from design BIM and labeled measured.
Topology-changing deviations
Physical topology and coordinates are different state channels.
A small coordinate change can cross a topology-sensitive release boundary.
Topology changes require rewrite and recertification rather than simple tolerance handling.
Entity and product reconciliation
Designed and field-observed entities require an explicit reconciliation relation.
Geometry alone does not prove identity-sensitive product claims.
Matching dimensions do not prove matching evidence.
Temporary-state safety
Final-state conformance does not prove temporary execution safety.
Safety follows through a physical trace when every action preserves the stage-safe invariant.
The same reasoning holds for sound set-valued physical enclosures.
Controlled invariance
Field safety can require active authorized recourse.
The quantifier order depends on what is observed before action selection.
Clairvoyant repairs are not executable field policies.
Nonanticipative field recourse
A field policy may depend only on evidence available when the action is chosen.
Scenario-wise repair existence is weaker than existence of one nonanticipative policy.
Approved recourse contracts must include domain, authority, actions, safety, terminal set, and claim coverage.
Approved versus unapproved field repairs
A pre-certified recourse policy can preserve covered claims without global redesign.
Field evidence must still establish that the actual deviation remained inside the recourse domain.
Out-of-contract repairs do not inherit affected assurance.
Such repairs trigger rewrite, rematerialization, sovereignty checking, and assurance recertification.
Incremental rematerialization and recertification
Complete physical dependency graphs support localized invalidation.
Dependency-directed physical recertification agrees with cold checking under the stated assumptions.
Unchanged complete physical reports form valid reconciliation frontiers.
Commissioning semantics
A commissioning test is a controlled physical experiment with explicit conditions and evidence.
Passing finite tests does not prove unrestricted operation.
Commissioning sufficiency is a partition-refinement property.
Commissioning coverage
Universal commissioning claims require direct coverage or a sound generalization theorem.
Model-assisted commissioning is sound under explicit model-validity and discrepancy hypotheses.
The discrepancy model itself remains an evidence obligation.
Physical release closure
Physical release is relative to a declared physical release manifest.
Every physical state still compatible with evidence must lie in accepted report cells.
Unique physical-state reconstruction is not required.
Mandatory conflicts and UNKNOWN block complete release.
Independent PCAB checking
A PCAB records physical evidence, calibration, knowledge sets, reconciliation, deviations, recourse, commissioning, and release claims.
The independent checker, not the reconstruction software, controls release.
PCAB checking is linear in explicit proof-DAG size plus local checker costs under the stated assumptions.
Prompt-9 measurement scheduling
Measurements become ordinary RCVOC computation actions.
The most precise measurement is not automatically the most valuable one.
Field measurement should be directed toward live release distinctions.
Prompt-10 physical red teaming
Event completeness, scan bias, calibration, product identity, recourse, commissioning, and release claims can be adversarially attacked.
Search failure is not proof of physical correctness.
Prompt-11 assurance integration
Physical evidence atoms enter the whole-project assurance graph.
Design assurance remains valid only within its physical applicability premises.
Physical release is a new assurance version.
Prompt-12 sovereignty integration
As-built architectural deviations may reopen protected-intent claims.
Authorized field recourse must preserve or explicitly amend sovereignty requirements.
Identical final geometry does not erase an unauthorized physical execution trace.
Prompt-13 materialization integration
Prompt-13 PCMP remains the authoritative design witness.
Prompt 14 determines which physical states remain consistent with actually observed execution.
Deviations outside Prompt-13 tolerance and recourse domains require rematerialization.
Whole-project proof-carrying physical execution
Under sound physical transition and observation models, the actual state remains inside the certified physical knowledge set.
Universal temporary and final reports over that set apply to the actual building.
Under the complete stated hypotheses, the actual building satisfies every declared physical-release obligation.
The theorem does not claim absence of unmodeled physical deviations.
The central result is:
[ \boxed{ \begin{minipage}{0.90\linewidth} Physical release does not require a fictional exact digital duplicate of reality. It requires a sound evidence-backed enclosure containing reality and lying entirely inside the accepted release semantics. \end{minipage} } ]



