82. What Has Now Been Proven
Operational twin semantics
The operational twin state is a set of augmented physical possibilities, not one preferred estimate.
The actual augmented state includes physical, degradation, fault, sensor, actuator, controller, maintenance, and evidence components.
The primary lifecycle invariant is [ z_t^\star \in \Gamma_t^{\mathrm{ODT}}. ]
Online set-membership filtering
Sound prediction and augmented observation updates preserve the actual operational state.
Multi-step soundness follows by induction.
Sensor-health uncertainty is included inside the observation relation.
Actuator-health uncertainty is included inside the transition relation.
Prediction and uncertainty growth
Operational prediction can broaden uncertainty.
Lifecycle knowledge is not monotone merely because time advances.
New evidence can shrink uncertainty, while dynamics and evidence expiry can expand it.
Claim synchronization
Exact microstate identification is unnecessary when every remaining state has one claim report.
The twin can remain useful even when many hidden physical states remain possible.
Claim desynchronization is the operationally relevant loss of certainty.
Sensor health and common cause
Sensors are uncertain physical assets, not external oracles.
Agreement among sensors does not establish independence.
Common calibration and reference failures must remain correlated.
Majority voting can fail under common cause.
Actuator health
A controller command is not the physical actuation.
Equal commands may produce unequal physical actions under different actuator states.
Safety must quantify over physically possible actuation.
Model families and validity
Several operational models may remain active simultaneously.
Model validity is set relative.
A mixed in-domain/out-of-domain knowledge set does not support one unconditional model claim.
Model discrepancy remains a separate evidence object.
Online parameter identification
Sound set-membership identification preserves the true parameter.
Drifting parameters require transition followed by observation intersection.
Exact parameter identification is unnecessary when the remaining parameter set is decision synchronized.
Model-learning authority
Predictive fit on finite data does not establish a universal physical enclosure.
Learned models can propose relations.
They become authoritative only through checked scope and enclosure guarantees.
Anomaly detection
A certified anomaly means every modeled healthy augmented state has been eliminated.
It does not identify the cause.
Empty healthy set and nonempty full set is distinct from total model/evidence inconsistency.
Fault detection and isolation
Fault hypotheses are projections of the augmented knowledge set.
Healthy, fault-present, isolated, and unresolved statuses are distinct.
A residual can be caused by plant, sensor, actuator, disturbance, or model discrepancy.
Detection is not isolation.
Decision-isolated diagnosis
Unique fault identity is unnecessary when all possible states require one intervention.
Immediate safe action can close while root-cause identity remains unresolved.
Fault isolation and maintenance-decision isolation are different properties.
Change-point detection
A change is certified when no unchanged trajectory remains compatible with evidence.
Change detection does not imply change localization.
Plant and sensor changes can remain observationally equivalent.
Passive and active diagnosis
Passive nondiagnosability does not imply active nondiagnosability.
A safe test input can separate modes that normal operation cannot.
Diagnostic experiments are physical interventions and must themselves be safe.
Diagnostic contextual equivalence
Diagnostic equivalence is Prompt-2 contextual equivalence specialized to safe operational experiments.
Diagnostic-equivalent fault states cannot be isolated by the current theory.
Disjoint outcome sets guarantee one-experiment discrimination.
Outcome-set inequality without disjointness is insufficient for guaranteed isolation.
Diagnostic-selection hardness
Minimum healthy-versus-fault distinguishing experiment selection is NP-hard.
Claim-directed diagnosis is therefore a genuine combinatorial optimization problem.
Degradation
Degradation is a relational state process, not automatically one scalar health index.
Maintenance can reduce, reset, replace, or introduce degradation uncertainty.
Different operational claims have different failure sets.
Reachable operational tubes
Outer-sound reach tubes contain every actual operational trajectory admitted by the theory.
Reach-tube inclusion in a safety set proves finite-horizon actual safety.
The result remains conditional on model, disturbance, fault, and actuation coverage.
Failure-time and RUL bounds
Failure time is a trajectory-relative quantity.
The actual failure time lies between the infimum and supremum over admitted trajectories.
RUL is claim relative.
A certified interval cannot be replaced by one unsupported point.
Maintenance semantics
Maintenance is a physical relation changing more than degradation.
Work-order existence does not prove the exact post-maintenance state.
Maintenance completion before the earliest possible failure can exclude pre-completion failure under explicit timing and safety hypotheses.
Replacement may change products, evidence, controls, and architecture.
Maintenance-planning hardness
Minimum-cost maintenance-closure planning is NP-hard in a restricted deterministic model.
Search, timing, access, and resource constraints can make practical planning harder.
Runtime safety shields
A robust safe command is safe for every current physical, fault, actuator, and disturbance possibility.
A checked shield can safely mediate untrusted control proposals.
Safety is independent of whether the proposer is learned, human, heuristic, or model predictive.
Safe controls may fail to exist.
Multi-step viability
One-step safety does not imply future viability.
The information-state viability kernel exactly characterizes existence of a finite-horizon safe nonanticipative policy.
Partial observation is handled at the knowledge-state level.
