50. Whole-Project Safe Transfer Theorem
THM-XFER23 — Whole-Project Proof-Carrying Cross-Project Learning and Target Recertification
Let:
(S) be a source fleet;
(B_\star) be a fixed target project;
(E_S) be accepted source evidence;
(E_\star) be accepted target evidence;
(\mathsf{PCTP}) be a checked transfer package.
Assume:
1. Source evidence soundness
Every source proposition used in transfer is supported by applicable evidence.
2. Identity and context integrity
Source and target projects, products, models, and versions are correctly identified.
3. Sampling semantics
Every statistical theorem uses the declared sampling unit and valid sampling assumptions.
4. Dependence semantics
Repeated, clustered, and common-cause observations are not treated as independent without justification.
5. Exact transfer discipline
Every deterministic target claim transferred from a source uses a sound exact bridge.
6. Statistical modality discipline
PAC, conformal, Bayesian, expected-risk, and population statements retain their exact modality.
7. Domain-shift discipline
Every shift-sensitive theorem checks its required support and invariance conditions.
8. Hierarchical set-transfer soundness
Every target set induced from fleet evidence uses a sound common-parameter bridge.
9. Distributionally robust soundness
Whenever a robust-distributional claim is interpreted as applying to the true target population, its true law belongs to the certified ambiguity set.
10. Approximate transfer ledger
Every quantitative transfer discrepancy is propagated through a checked error calculus.
11. Model and PCPE applicability
Transferred PCPEs, fault models, degradation models, and diagnostic models pass target validity checks.
12. Theory-expansion discipline
New applicable source mechanisms trigger dependency-complete target recertification.
13. Target evidence
Every target claim requiring target-local evidence receives it.
14. Assurance integration
Transferred artifacts enter target release only through Prompt-11 assurance.
15. Sovereignty
No transferred policy or optimizer silently changes target architectural authority.
16. UNKNOWN discipline
No unresolved bridge, support claim, shift claim, or statistical premise is promoted.
Then:
Exact-transfer conclusion
Every target claim classified as
[ \mathsf{EXACT_TARGET} ]
or
[ \mathsf{EXACT_TRANSPORT} ]
is true under its declared target theory.
Set-valued conclusion
Every target parameter or state classified as
[ \mathsf{SET_VALUED_TARGET} ]
contains the true target quantity under its set-membership assumptions.
PAC conclusion
Every PAC certificate satisfies exactly its high-probability population-risk statement.
Conformal conclusion
Every conformal certificate satisfies exactly its exchangeability-based marginal coverage statement.
Bayesian conclusion
Every Bayesian certificate is a valid conditional consequence of its declared prior and likelihood model, with no automatic deterministic or frequentist escalation.
Robust-distributional conclusion
Every robust-distributional claim holds for every law in its certified ambiguity set, including the true target law when membership is established.
Non-escalation conclusion
No statistical, Bayesian, heuristic, or source-only statement becomes a deterministic target claim without a checked conversion rule.
Release conclusion
Whole-project target release remains governed by Prompt-11 Proof-Carrying Release.
Proof
Proceed by modality.
Exact transfer
Source facts are sound by assumption 1.
Bridges are sound by assumption 5.
THM-XFER2 proves each direct exact transfer.
THM-XFER3 proves sound composition.
THM-XFER4 proves report reuse under contextual equivalence.
Thus exact target claims are true.
Set-valued transfer
Assumption 8 permits application of THM-XFER13.
Therefore the true target parameter belongs to each certified transferred target set.
Target evidence may refine the set through COR-XFER2.
If a universal target proof holds over the set, COR-XFER3 yields an exact target claim.
PAC claims
Under assumption 3, THM-XFER5 supplies the stated high-probability simultaneous population-risk event.
No fixed-target conclusion is added.
