65. Falsification and Regression Suite
No bridge: identical source evidence, two target truths. Exact transfer must fail.
Exact bridge: a finite exact source–target relation must permit deterministic transfer.
Bridge composition: composed certificates must equal direct relational reasoning.
Contextual equivalence: equivalent states preserve admitted reports.
Duplicated sample: repeated correlated records must not pass as IID units.
PAC finite class: simulations must match the simultaneous bound’s statistical frequency.
Population versus target: high population success with one known failing fixed target.
Conformal exchangeability: empirical marginal coverage on an exchangeable benchmark.
Conformal subgroup: marginal success with subgroup failure.
Covariate shift: exact finite weighted-risk identity.
Support gap: target-only state with unconstrained outcome.
Selection bias: observed fleet contains no failures while population does.
Simpson reversal: pooled ranking reverses both within-context rankings.
Mechanism transport: invariant kernel plus target covariates reproduces target distribution.
False causal transport: identical observational distribution, different interventions.
Hierarchical transfer: true target parameter remains inside the induced fleet set.
Fleet monotonicity: added compatible source evidence shrinks the fixed-theory set.
Theory expansion: new source fault enlarges target fault family and reopens claims.
Universal fleet breaker: one source refutes universal class claim but not target-specific claim.
Distributional robustness: every law in a finite ambiguity set respects the robust bound.
Approximate transfer: verify the Lipschitz error calculation.
PCPE transfer: source containment plus monotone map preserves target containment.
Wrong PCPE target: changed product or control regime invalidates transfer.
Federated evidence: local packets support a checked aggregate.
Federated privacy negative: raw-data update yields no privacy claim.
Modality gate: PAC evidence remains statistical.
Target measurement: fleet target set intersects soundly with local evidence.
Minimum source selection: recover a known Weighted Set Cover optimum.
Incremental transfer: incremental and cold checking agree.
Cross-project red team: source success and target failure.
Prompt-9 integration: target-critical measurement outranks irrelevant fleet refinement.
Prompt-10 integration: source breaker creates a checked theory-expansion event.
Prompt-11 integration: statistical evidence remains a typed statistical proposition.
Prompt-12 integration: fleet preferences do not override target architectural authority.
Prompt-13 integration: product transfer requires exact applicability.
Prompt-14 integration: fleet sensor evidence does not replace target calibration.
Prompt-15 integration: transferred fault theory expands hypotheses without asserting actual fault.
PCTP independence: checker verifies the package without trusting learner internals.



