75. What Has Now Been Proven
Project and fleet semantics
A fixed observed fleet is not automatically a statistical population.
Project identities, contexts, versions, and evidence remain source scoped.
A fixed target and a random target variable have different semantics.
Source truth alone has no general target implication.
Fixed fleets versus populations
Exact fleet counts are statements about the observed fleet.
Population claims require an explicit probability space.
Population probability does not determine one arbitrary target’s truth.
Sampling units and dependence
Sampling units must be declared.
Repeated rows do not create independent evidence.
Shared calibration, firmware, climate, and control systems can induce dependence.
Perfectly correlated repeats retain the variance of one observation.
Exact transport bridges
Exact deterministic transfer is sound under a checked source–target relation.
Exact transport certificates compose.
Scope and version compatibility are required.
Contextual-equivalence reuse
Cross-project states that are contextually equivalent under the target experiment theory have equal reports.
Product labels and geometric similarity do not establish that equivalence.
Exchangeability
Exchangeability is a distributional permutation symmetry.
IID implies exchangeability.
Exchangeability does not imply physical identity or independence.
PAC generalization
Hoeffding concentration was derived from an MGF bound.
A simultaneous finite-class PAC theorem was proved.
Data-dependent model selection remains covered by the simultaneous event.
The result is a high-probability population-risk claim.
Conformal prediction
Exchangeable-score ranks are uniform with explicit random tie breaking.
Finite-sample split-conformal marginal coverage was proved.
The result is marginal rather than fixed-target deterministic.
Marginal coverage can coexist with zero subgroup coverage.
Population versus target truth
High population reliability can include a failing fixed target.
Population claims cannot be silently promoted to exact target claims.
Domain shift
Covariate, conditional, support, and selection shift are distinct.
Covariate-shift risk equals a source weighted expectation under exact assumptions.
Conditional invariance is load bearing.
Support overlap
Target behavior outside source support is not identified by source evidence alone.
Perfect source performance can coexist with arbitrarily poor target performance.
Selection and survivorship bias
Observed failure rates can be arbitrarily distorted by uncontrolled selection.
Observing only successful projects can make observed failure rate zero.
Observing only failed projects can make it one.
Context confounding and aggregation
Pooled fleet statistics can reverse every within-context comparison.
Context must be represented before aggregation is interpreted.
The reversal does not alone establish a causal explanation.
Conditional and causal mechanism transport
An invariant conditional kernel plus target context distribution determines the target outcome distribution.
The mechanism-invariance premise is itself a proof obligation.
Observational equivalence does not identify intervention behavior.
Hierarchical set-membership fleet learning
Fleet evidence can define a deterministic common-hyperparameter set.
A checked target bridge induces a sound target-parameter set.
The true target parameter remains in that set.
Additional sound source constraints shrink the set under a fixed theory.
Target-local evidence may refine it further.
A universal proof over the target set can discharge an exact target claim.
Theory expansion from fleet discoveries
New fleet evidence can enlarge the target fault or mechanism theory.
Old universal target proofs do not automatically survive the expansion.
More fleet evidence can reopen target assurance.
This is epistemic progress rather than inconsistency.
Source Claim Breakers
One source breaker refutes a universal class claim when the source belongs to the class.
It does not prove another target project fails.
A bridged source breaker may justify expanding target fault theory.
Distributionally robust transfer
Robust risk bounds are sound for every law in the ambiguity set.
The true target law must be shown to belong to that set.
Robust distributional risk remains a population-law statement.
Approximate transfer error calculus
Source approximation and transfer discrepancy compose through triangle and Lipschitz bounds.
Mandatory exact semantic channels remain noncompensatory.
PCPE transfer
A source PCPE can transfer through a monotone set map and explicit discrepancy envelope.
Source validity does not automatically imply target validity.
Product, interface, control, and context compatibility remain required.
Fault and degradation transfer
Fault models may transfer as exact, set-valued, statistical, Bayesian, or heuristic objects.
Source fault occurrence does not prove target fault presence.
Source fault discovery can expand target hypotheses.
Federated evidence
Locally certified evidence packets compose through a sound aggregation rule.
Raw data need not be centralized for that inference.
Federated architecture does not itself prove privacy.
Claim modalities
Exact, set-valued, PAC, conformal, Bayesian, robust-distributional, finite-fleet, and heuristic claims remain distinct.
Generic modality escalation is unsound.
Valid conversion requires a separate theorem.
Target-specific recertification
A target gate checks identity, context, support, bridge, evidence, modality, validity, and sovereignty.
Target-specific evidence remains necessary when the transferred object does not close the target claim.
Statistical fleet evidence does not bypass target assurance.
Transfer-evidence selection hardness
Minimum-cost transfer-evidence selection is NP-hard.
Selecting which source artifacts to verify is itself an optimization problem.
Incremental transfer recertification
Complete dependency graphs permit exact localized invalidation.
Incremental and cold transfer checking agree under the stated assumptions.
Prompt-9 RCVOC integration
Fleet learning, bridge validation, source inspection, and target measurement become metareasoning actions.
Prompt 9 remains the scheduler.
Target-critical evidence may have more value than broad fleet learning.
Prompt-10 adversarial integration
Transfer assumptions can be red-teamed.
Source success and target failure form a transfer breaker.
Novel fleet failures become checked theory-expansion events.
Prompt-11 assurance integration
Statistical certificates enter assurance as typed statistical claims.
Exact target release remains governed by ordinary assurance derivations.
Source evidence cannot manufacture a release conclusion outside its modality.
Prompt-12 sovereignty integration
Fleet optimization or preference models cannot override target authority.
Cross-project preference evidence remains advisory unless governed.
Prompt-13 materialization integration
Product and subsystem transfer requires exact materialization applicability.
Equal nominal roles do not establish product equivalence.
Prompt-14 physical evidence integration
Fleet calibration evidence does not replace target calibration evidence.
Cross-project geometry or sensor facts remain project scoped.
Prompt-15 operational-twin integration
Fleet fault theories may enlarge a target PCOT’s hypothesis set.
Fleet parameter sets may initialize target set-membership estimation.
Target operational claims remain synchronized only through target evidence and valid bridges.
Proof-Carrying Transfer Packages
A PCTP records every source, assumption, bridge, modality, shift condition, and target gate result.
The independent checker, not the learner, controls target authority.
Explicit PCTP checking is linear in certificate-DAG size plus local check costs.
Whole-project safe cross-project learning
Exact target claims follow only through exact bridges or target proofs.
Set-valued target claims contain truth under common-mechanism assumptions.
PAC certificates retain their population-risk semantics.
Conformal certificates retain marginal coverage semantics.
Bayesian statements remain conditional on their model.
Robust-distributional claims remain law-level claims.
Heuristic transfer remains nonauthoritative.
Whole-project release remains a target-specific Prompt-11 conclusion.
The central result is:
[ \boxed{ \begin{minipage}{0.9\linewidth} ANANKE can accumulate reusable engineering knowledge across projects without pretending that projects are interchangeable. The logical modality of every transferred object must remain explicit, and exact target authority arises only from a checked source–target bridge, a sound target set containing truth, or a target-specific proof. \end{minipage} } ]



