65. What Has Now Been Proven
Reliability semantics
Reliability is defined relative to a target, mission, policy, horizon, failure semantics, and stochastic law.
Failure probability is one minus mission reliability under the declared convention.
A target trajectory law is distinct from a fleet population law.
Law uncertainty is distinct from randomness inside one law.
Deterministic safety versus reliability
Reliability below one leaves an admissible failure trajectory.
A high probability of success is not logical impossibility of failure.
Probabilistic reliability cannot silently replace deterministic safety.
Failure time and hazard
Survival equals mission reliability.
Under absolute continuity, survival is the exponential of negative cumulative hazard.
Hazard is not a failure probability.
Availability and repairable systems
Mission reliability concerns no first failure.
Point availability concerns being operational at a time.
Long-run availability concerns time occupancy or limiting behavior.
The two-state repairable CTMC has explicitly different reliability and availability formulas.
Availability can remain high while mission reliability tends to zero.
Finite CTMC reliability
Mission reliability is obtained from the killed up-state generator.
Repair transitions after first failure do not enter no-first-failure reliability.
Uniformization
A finite generator admits a Poisson-weighted discrete-chain representation.
Uniformization truncation carries an exact Poisson-tail numerical enclosure.
The enclosure is independently checkable.
Coherent failure logic
Every finite coherent-system failure contains a minimal cut.
The system failure event is the union of minimal-cut events.
Cut sets
A minimal-cut union bound requires no independence.
Independent component failure permits product intersection probabilities.
Overlapping cut events still prevent naïve exact summation.
Series and parallel systems
Independent series reliability is a product.
Independent parallel reliability is one minus the product of failure probabilities.
These formulas are conditional on independence.
Dependence and common cause
Equal component marginals do not determine system reliability.
Perfect common cause can change parallel failure probability from (p^2) to (p).
Conditional common-cause models require mixture integration over the shared cause.
Dynamic fault semantics
Terminal failed-component sets cannot encode every order-sensitive failure claim.
Dynamic reliability may require timed traces and mode state.
Competing risks
Independent exponential competing risks combine into one exponential first-failure time.
First-cause probabilities are proportional to their rates.
These formulas require independence and exponential waiting times.
Computational hardness
Exact reliability of arbitrary compact Boolean failure logic is #P-hard.
Minimum cut-set hardening is NP-hard.
Small fault logic does not guarantee easy exact probability evaluation.
Adversarial robustness versus probability
No generic monotone relationship exists between robustness radius and reliability.
A nearby breaker can be extremely improbable.
A distant breaker can have large probability.
Zero-failure evidence
Zero observed failures do not prove zero failure probability.
Under IID Bernoulli trials, zero failures give an exact one-sided upper exclusion threshold.
Dependence invalidates the IID calculation unless modeled.
Naïve rare-event Monte Carlo
The Monte Carlo estimator is unbiased under IID target-law simulation.
Its relative variance is approximately (1/(Np)) for rare (p).
Rare-event precision can require sample size proportional to (1/p).
Monte Carlo confidence is about estimator error, not realized physical success.
Importance sampling
Importance sampling is unbiased under failure-region support coverage.
Its variance is determined by the second moment of the likelihood-weighted failure measure.
The conditional-failure distribution is the formal zero-variance proposal.
That ideal law is not generally constructively available.
Missing positive-probability failure support invalidates ordinary importance sampling.
Fragility and hazard
Fragility is conditional failure probability given hazard and context.
Target failure probability is obtained by integrating fragility against the target hazard law.
Fragility alone is not hazard frequency.
Return periods and extremes
Under IID annual exceedances, waiting time is geometric with mean (1/p).
Equal annual marginals do not determine return-time behavior under dependence.
A return period is not a deterministic calendar interval.
Finite observations do not identify an unrestricted far tail.
Tail risk
Expected loss does not determine catastrophic tails.
VaR may ignore severity beyond its quantile.
CVaR is monotone under almost-sure loss ordering.
VaR and CVaR remain probabilistic loss functionals rather than physical safety guarantees.
Distributionally robust reliability
A reliability ambiguity set yields lower and upper reliability bounds.
If the true law belongs to the set, its reliability lies inside the envelope.
Shrinking the law set tightens the envelope.
Theory expansion may lower the certified reliability bound.
Graceful degradation
Service reliability can be defined at a required service floor.
Higher required service cannot increase reliability.
Binary failure is not the only meaningful service semantics.
Resilience
Resilience can retain minimum service, recovery time, and cumulative deficit as separate coordinates.
Universal reach-tube bounds apply to the actual trajectory.
Probabilistic resilience remains probabilistic.
Reliability and resilience are independent dimensions.
Recovery
Recovery actions and policies are state-changing physical processes.
Shared repair resources invalidate naïve composition of local recovery times.
Properly timed safe maintenance can exclude failure before maintenance completion under explicit assumptions.
Cascading failure
One failure may change the future failure law of other components.
Static independent nominal failure rates can miss load-redistribution cascades.
Online ODT reliability
The Prompt-15 ODT initializes current target reliability from current target evidence.
A robust lower reliability bound applies when the true future law lies in the current law family.
Exact physical microstate identification is not required to bound mission reliability.
Reliability assurance leases
A reliability bound can remain valid through a future horizon while model, policy, evidence, and law assumptions remain active.
Lease expiry reopens the claim.
A reliability lease is a probabilistic assurance object.
Fleet reliability transfer
Fleet evidence may refine target parameter sets or law families.
Fleet probabilities remain population claims unless validly transported.
New fleet failures may expand the target theory.
Theory expansion can reduce target reliability lower bounds.
Prompt-9 integration
Rare-event simulations, common-cause checks, hazard refinements, and repairs become RCVOC actions.
Prompt 9 remains the computation scheduler.
Prompt-10 integration
Probabilistic reliability and adversarial robustness remain distinct.
Reliability assumptions can be attacked by checked Claim Breakers.
Prompt-11 integration
Reliability certificates enter assurance as typed probabilistic evidence.
Deterministic safety roots still require deterministic proof.
Prompt-12 integration
Reliability optimization cannot override protected architecture or authority.
Prompt-13 integration
Reliability hardening that changes products or topology requires rematerialization checking.
Prompt-14 integration
As-built identity determines applicability of component reliability models.
Prompt-15 integration
Fault, degradation, sensor, actuator, and maintenance states initialize current target reliability.
Prompt-16 integration
Fleet reliability evidence retains exact transfer modality.
Population bounds do not become exact target reliability facts.
New fleet mechanisms can reopen target reliability.
PCRR
A PCRR records mission, law, dependence, common cause, failure logic, rare-event methods, repair, resilience, and evidence.
The independent checker, not the model generator, controls authority.
Whole-project assurance
Exact numerical enclosures remain exact numerical claims.
Robust target reliability remains a probability bound under a law family.
Availability remains distinct from no-first-failure reliability.
Rare-event estimates retain estimator uncertainty.
Tail-risk claims retain their loss semantics.
Deterministic resilience and probabilistic resilience remain distinct.
