The deterministic-safety law is:
[ \boxed{ \textbf{Probability close to one is still probability, not logical impossibility of failure.} } ]
The dependence law is:
[ \boxed{ \textbf{The reliability of a system is determined by its joint failure structure, not by a list of component failure rates.} } ]
The redundancy law is:
[ \boxed{ \textbf{Redundancy is valuable only to the extent that the redundant paths do not share the failure mechanism that defeats them.} } ]
The rare-event law is:
[ \boxed{ \textbf{The rarer the event, the less acceptable it becomes to mistake not observing it for proving it impossible.} } ]
The simulation law is:
[ \boxed{ \textbf{Monte Carlo produces statistical evidence about a probability model. It does not turn a stochastic claim into a deterministic one.} } ]
The importance-sampling law is:
[ \boxed{ \textbf{Bias the simulation toward failure when useful—but carry the likelihood ratio and prove that no relevant failure region disappeared.} } ]
The fragility law is:
[ \boxed{ \textbf{Fragility describes conditional failure given hazard. Reliability also requires the hazard law.} } ]
The return-period law is:
[ \boxed{ \textbf{A return period is a property of a stochastic recurrence model, not an appointment on a calendar.} } ]
The tail law is:
[ \boxed{ \textbf{Two designs with the same expected loss can have radically different catastrophic tails. Preserve tail semantics explicitly.} } ]
The resilience law is:
[ \boxed{ \textbf{Reliability asks whether service fails. Resilience asks what happens after disruption and how the system recovers. Do not collapse them.} } ]
The recovery law is:
[ \boxed{ \textbf{Recovery is a coupled physical process with crews, access, materials, dependencies, and time—not a scalar repair-rate annotation.} } ]
The cascade law is:
[ \boxed{ \textbf{When one failure changes the conditions under which the next failure occurs, reliability belongs to the trajectory, not merely the terminal component set.} } ]
The fleet law is:
[ \boxed{ \textbf{Fleet evidence can improve target reliability theory, and sometimes its most important contribution is discovering that the old target reliability theory was incomplete.} } ]
The operational law is:
[ \boxed{ \textbf{Reliability should be conditioned on what ANANKE currently knows about the actual building, not computed forever from the commissioning-day state.} } ]
The assurance law is:
[ \boxed{ \textbf{A reliability certificate proves exactly its probability statement under exactly its stochastic theory. It proves no stronger claim merely because the numerical probability looks impressive.} } ]
The deepest Prompt-17 principle is:
[ \boxed{ \begin{minipage}{0.91\linewidth} ANANKE becomes a true reliability system only when it can reason simultaneously about failure probability, dependence, common cause, rare hazards, repair, degradation, cascading effects, service loss, recovery, statistical estimation, model uncertainty, and deterministic safety without confusing any of them. The objective is not to manufacture reassuring reliability numbers. The objective is to make every statement about uncertain failure and recovery carry the exact stochastic theory, evidence, dependence structure, numerical certificate, and target-specific assumptions that make that statement true. \end{minipage} } ]
