48. Falsification and Regression Suite
Exact partition: a disjoint quantity cover must reproduce the authoritative total.
Double counting: assign one entity to two line items; the checker must reject exact completeness.
Omission: remove one cost-bearing entity; the ledger must report incomplete coverage.
Unit mismatch: combine incompatible dimensions without conversion; reject.
Expired quote: current price cannot be certified from an expired quote.
Quantity–price correlation: exact joint set must beat the false Cartesian product.
Budget under: place the full cost set below the budget.
Budget over: place the full cost set above the budget.
Budget mixed: make the enclosure cross the budget; return unresolved.
Carbon functional-unit mismatch: reject area multiplied directly by a mass-based factor.
Carbon budget: verify three-way classification.
Critical path: compare THM-ECO7 against exhaustive precedence-only schedules.
Shared crane: critical-path schedule must fail resource checking.
RCPSP PARTITION benchmark: recover the partition equivalence.
Expected-duration counterexample: verify THM-ECO10.
Schedule nonanticipativity: scenario-wise schedules exist but no common policy.
Supplier common cause: equal marginals, different joint procurement failure.
Inventory deficit: detect the first negative balance.
Invalid substitute: same capacity or geometry, incompatible evidence.
Valid substitute: complete contextual equivalence preserves reports.
Cash prefix violation: positive final balance but negative prefix.
Financing repair: add valid credit and verify feasibility changes.
Procurement recourse inside contract: preserve covered claims.
Procurement recourse outside contract: require recertification.
Buildable but unprocurable artifact: materialization remains while execution fiber is empty.
Conflict core: recover an analytically known minimal core.
Incremental versus cold: require identical economic reports.
Prompt-9 integration: a quote acquisition should outrank an irrelevant schedule refinement when the quote blocks release.
Prompt-10 integration: insert a hidden common supplier dependency and produce a Claim Breaker.
Prompt-11 integration: authentic but inapplicable cost/carbon evidence must not support release.
Prompt-12 integration: a cheaper design change violating protected architecture must not be committed.
Prompt-13 integration: product substitution outside equivalence triggers rematerialization.
Prompt-14 integration: ordered product without field delivery evidence must not be labeled installed.
Prompt-15 integration: lifecycle replacement must update future cost and product identity.
Prompt-16 integration: fleet unit costs remain statistical or set-valued.
Prompt-17 integration: supplier reliability remains probabilistic.
PCEE independence: independent checker verifies the package without optimization history.



