59. What Has Now Been Proven
Quantity semantics
A disjoint complete quantity cover aggregates exactly under finite additivity.
Exact quantity aggregation depends on correct scope, units, lineage, and version.
Sound quantity intervals aggregate to a sound total interval.
Quantity aggregation requires no probability or independence assumption.
Correlation can make interval aggregation conservative.
Cost evidence
Quotes, contracts, forecasts, historical actuals, estimates, and allowances are different modalities.
A quote is scoped to product, quantity, place, terms, currency, and validity interval.
An expired quote does not establish a current purchase price.
Authenticity of a quote does not imply current applicability.
Joint cost uncertainty
Quantity and price marginals do not determine their joint cost relation.
Unjustified Cartesian products can produce false low and high costs.
The project cost object is a set or law over complete economic worlds.
A point estimate is not the project cost set.
Budget closure
If every possible covered cost is below budget, the project is certified under budget.
If the certified lower cost bound exceeds budget, it is certified over budget.
A cost enclosure crossing the budget remains unresolved.
Budget closure is a governance-relative decision.
Carbon semantics
A carbon factor requires exact product scope and functional unit.
Functional-unit mismatch invalidates naïve multiplication.
Quantity–factor dependence must be preserved.
Carbon budgets admit the same sound three-way classification as monetary budgets.
Embodied and operational carbon are distinct.
Combining embodied and operational carbon requires an accounting horizon and rule.
Construction activities
Construction activities carry physical transformation, duration, resource, material, workspace, and evidence semantics.
An activity network must correspond to legal Prompt-13 construction actions.
Critical-path scheduling
Under precedence-only assumptions, minimum makespan equals longest path length.
The earliest-start schedule constructively attains the critical-path bound.
The critical path remains a lower bound after resources are added.
Resource-constrained scheduling
Finite resources can make the critical path unattainable.
Two parallel crane activities may require sequential execution.
Restricted two-capacity resource-constrained makespan minimization is weakly NP-hard.
A legal construction sequence does not prove deadline-feasible scheduling.
Schedule uncertainty
The critical path of expected durations need not equal expected project completion.
Joint duration scenarios matter.
Expected inputs do not generally commute with makespan.
Nonanticipative scheduling
Scenario-wise clairvoyant schedules need not form one executable policy.
A valid adaptive policy must respect the information available when each commitment is made.
Robust universal feasibility implies scenario-wise feasibility, but not conversely.
Procurement semantics
Ordered, shipped, delivered, accepted, and installed states are distinct.
Historical average lead time is not a delivery commitment.
Material must be available before the activity needing it.
Delivery claims retain exact, contractual, set-valued, or probabilistic modality.
Supplier common cause
Two supplier marginals do not determine redundant procurement reliability.
Two suppliers can share one factory, port, carrier, or subcomponent.
Common-cause failure can change joint supply failure from (p^2) to (p).
Inventory
Material inventory obeys a conservation balance.
A trace requiring negative inventory is infeasible.
Physical material delivery and accepted usable inventory are distinct.
Product substitution
Equal price does not establish valid substitution.
Equal geometry does not establish valid substitution.
Complete materialization-and-economic contextual equivalence does establish report-preserving substitution.
Non-equivalent substitutes require recertification.
Procurement recourse
A procurement contingency is valid only within its declared disruption domain.
Procurement recourse must be nonanticipative.
Covered recourse preserves exactly the claims universally proved by its certificate.
Outside-domain improvisation does not inherit old assurance.
Supplier-selection hardness
Minimum-cost supplier coverage is NP-hard.
Supplier selection may require more than nominal product coverage once timing and common cause are added.
Cash flow
Cash balances equal the initial balance plus receipt/outflow prefixes.
Nonnegative prefix balances exactly characterize deterministic liquidity feasibility.
Positive final net cash does not imply the project can survive intermediate stages.
Financing semantics must be represented explicitly.
Discounted cost
Present value requires a declared discount curve.
Discounting does not alter physical or schedule feasibility.
Discounted monetary value cannot erase carbon, safety, or sovereignty constraints.
Cost and carbon tradeoffs
Lower cost does not imply lower embodied carbon.
Lower carbon does not imply lower cost.
Cost, schedule, carbon, reliability, and sovereignty need not admit one weighted objective.
Economic execution fibers
Economic execution feasibility is represented by a nonempty execution fiber.
The fiber is modality specific.
A deterministic witness, robust fixed plan, adaptive policy, and probabilistic bound are different objects.
A physically buildable artifact can have an empty current execution fiber.
Execution conflict cores
Economic infeasibility can be localized to a constraint core.
A conflict core does not determine which constraint should be relaxed.
Repairs may alter supplier, product, resource, deadline, financing, logistics, or design.
Repair frontiers
Incomparable repair consequences should be returned as a Pareto frontier.
Protected design changes require Prompt-12 authority.
Prompt-9 integration
Quotes, quantity checks, schedule solves, carbon verification, and supplier checks become RCVOC computations.
Prompt 9 remains the computation scheduler.
Prompt-10 integration
