56. Falsification and Regression Suite
Central optimum not equilibrium: verify the coordination-defection game.
DSIC inequality checker: detect one profitable report deviation.
DSIC implies BIC: exhaustively verify under several priors.
BIC not DSIC: use the perfectly correlated type example.
Revelation transformation: compare indirect equilibrium and truthful direct outcomes.
DSIC report not evidence: false report remains weakly dominant.
Ex post IR implies interim IR: finite exhaustive check.
Interim IR implies ex ante IR: finite expectation check.
Ex ante without interim IR: use ((-1,3)) type utilities.
Interim without ex post IR: use ((1,-1)) state utilities.
Reverse-auction truthfulness: exhaustive bid thresholds.
Qualification separation: low unqualified bid must not win.
Single-parameter monotonicity: detect increasing allocation in cost.
VCG truthfulness: enumerate every report deviation in a finite model.
VCG budget limitation: find a VCG instance not strongly budget balanced.
Bilateral-trade impossibility: verify payment contradiction.
Adverse-selection menu: each type self-selects correctly.
Moral-hazard threshold: verify bonus inequality.
Uninformative signal: confirm no outcome contract induces high effort.
Limited-liability minimum bonus: compare all bounded wage pairs.
Observability/verifiability distinction: private observation lacks contract evidence.
Hold-up underinvestment: verify (\beta S<k\le S).
Bargaining split: maximize Nash product exhaustively.
Multiple Nash equilibria: coordination game.
Weak versus full implementation: desired outcome appears in only one equilibrium.
Noncredible threat: normal-form Nash fails subgame check.
SPNE removal: backward induction chooses accommodation.
Coalition deviation: reverse-auction bid inflation.
Coalition count: verify (2^n-1).
Contract automaton valid transition: accepted event reaches amended state.
Invalid contract event: wrong-version event is rejected.
Obligation without performance: active obligation and failed delivery.
Performance without authorization: physical work exists with no valid amendment.
Valid change order: all approvals and dependencies pass.
Invalid change order: missing authority blocks transition.
Causal/contractual responsibility split: same cause, different risk allocation.
Responsibility closure: all dispute worlds assign one party.
Responsibility nonidentifiability: two surviving narratives disagree.
Prompt-12 authority rejection: efficient mechanism outcome violates protected intent.
Prompt-13 rematerialization: change order alters product/interface geometry.
Prompt-18 transfer infeasibility: balanced mechanism requires unavailable cash.
Prompt-19 expired action: mechanism selects an option after expiry.
Prompt-17 reported reliability: incentive-compatible report lacks reliability evidence.
Prompt-16 fleet modality: population supplier data do not identify one supplier’s type.
Prompt-10 coalition breaker: hidden side agreement invalidates coalition claim.
Incremental versus cold checking: exact agreement after contract change.
AI mechanism rejection: profitable deviation defeats an AI-proposed mechanism.
PCGC independence: checker validates the package without learner or negotiator history.



57. Proof-Assistant Formalization
Project agent
structure Agent where
  Type : Type
  Message : Type
  Action : Type
  utility :
    Outcome →
    ℝ →
    Type →
    ℝ
Direct mechanism
structure DirectMechanism
    (N : Type)
    [Fintype N]
    (Ty : N → Type) where
  outcome :
    (∀ i, Ty i) →
    Outcome
  transfer :
    (∀ i, Ty i) →
    N →
    ℝ
DSIC
def DSIC
    (M : DirectMechanism N Ty) : Prop :=
  ∀ i θ θ' θminus,
    utilityAt M i θ θ θminus
      ≥
    utilityAt M i θ θ' θminus
A full implementation must define how (\theta_{-i}) is assembled with the reported type.
Finite BIC
def BIC
    (M : DirectMechanism N Ty)
    (prior : FinitePrior Ty) : Prop :=
  ∀ i θ θ',
    conditionalExpectedUtility
      M prior i θ θ
      ≥
    conditionalExpectedUtility
      M prior i θ θ'
Ex post IR
def ExPostIR
    (M : DirectMechanism N Ty)
    (outside : ∀ i, Ty i → ℝ) : Prop :=
  ∀ reports i,
    truthfulUtility M reports i
      ≥
    outside i (reports i)
Strategic implementation fiber
def ImplementationFiber
    (target : Outcome)
    (mode : GovMode) :
    Set (Mechanism × StrategyProfile) :=
  {ms |
    Equilibrium mode ms.1 ms.2 ∧
    InducedOutcome ms.1 ms.2 = target}
Contract automaton
structure ContractMachine where
  State : Type
  Event : Type
  initial : State
  step :
    State →
    Event →
    Set State
  obligations :
    State →
    Set Obligation
  rights :
    State →
    Set Right
  version : Version
Dispute fiber
def DisputeFiber
    (C : ContractMachine)
    (E : EvidenceState) :
    Set ProjectWorld :=
  {w |
    ContractConsistent C w ∧
    EvidenceConsistent E w}
Responsibility closure
def ResponsibilityClosed
    (fiber : Set ProjectWorld)
    (i : AgentId) : Prop :=
  ∀ w ∈ fiber,
    responsibility w = i
Proof-Carrying Governance and Contract Package
structure PCGC where
  project : ProjectId
  agents : AgentStore
  mechanism : MechanismArtifact
  contracts : ContractStore
  incentiveCerts :
    IncentiveCertificateStore
  participationCerts :
    ParticipationCertificateStore
  monitoring :
    MonitoringModel
  responsibility :
    ResponsibilityModel
  disputes :
    DisputeStore
  dependencies :
    DependencyGraph
  version : Version
DSIC implies BIC skeleton
theorem dsic_implies_bic
    (hDSIC : DSIC M)
    (prior : FinitePrior Ty) :
    BIC M prior := by
  intro i θ θ'
  apply conditionalExpectation_mono
  intro θminus
  exact hDSIC i θ θ' θminus
Responsibility closure soundness skeleton
theorem closed_responsibility_sound
    (actual : ProjectWorld)
    (hActual :
      actual ∈ DisputeFiber C E)
    (hClosed :
      ResponsibilityClosed
        (DisputeFiber C E)
        i) :
    responsibility actual = i := by
  exact hClosed actual hActual



