67. What Has Now Been Proven
Strategic implementation
A centrally feasible outcome need not be an equilibrium.
A centralized social optimum need not be implemented by the fixed governance mechanism.
Strategic implementability is relative to a mechanism, strategy profile, and equilibrium mode.
Weak implementation and full implementation are distinct.
Existence of one good equilibrium does not guarantee the desired outcome in every equilibrium.
Agent and type semantics
Strategic claims require explicit agents, types, actions, messages, utilities, information, and authority.
A type report is not itself evidence of the underlying physical fact.
Rationality claims are model scoped.
DSIC and BIC
DSIC is a pointwise deviation condition.
BIC is an expected conditional deviation condition.
DSIC implies BIC under every compatible prior.
BIC does not imply DSIC.
Off-support report contingencies can defeat dominant-strategy truthfulness while preserving Bayesian truthfulness.
Revelation principle
A finite indirect Bayesian equilibrium can be reproduced by a truthful direct mechanism.
The revelation transformation preserves the equilibrium outcome distribution.
The revelation principle does not establish DSIC, uniqueness, coalition resistance, or physical truth.
Incentive compatibility and evidence
A DSIC mechanism can permit false reports as weakly dominant strategies.
Incentive compatibility does not replace evidence verification.
Participation
Ex post IR implies interim IR.
Interim IR implies ex ante IR.
Neither converse holds.
Positive total surplus does not guarantee voluntary participation.
DSIC does not imply IR.
Procurement mechanisms
DSIC single-parameter procurement requires nonincreasing allocation in reported cost.
Truthful cost reporting is weakly dominant in the qualified reverse second-price auction.
Price truthfulness does not establish qualification, capacity, lead time, or reliability.
VCG
Finite quasilinear Groves mechanisms are DSIC.
VCG truthfulness relies on quasilinear utility and exact welfare maximization.
VCG truthfulness does not imply budget balance or coalition-proofness.
Mechanism tradeoffs
Efficiency, DSIC, ex post IR, and strong budget balance can be mutually incompatible in finite bilateral trade.
Mechanism budget balance does not imply project cash-flow feasibility.
Formal contract outcome need not equal social optimum.
Adverse selection
Private precontract information requires type-dependent IC constraints.
Screening menus can induce type self-selection under a declared finite model.
Screening success remains type-model dependent.
Moral hazard
Hidden high effort is incentivized exactly when:
[ (p_H-p_L)(w_1-w_0)\ge c. ]
Outcome-contingent pay cannot incentivize costly effort when output is uninformative.
Under limited liability, the minimum success bonus has a closed-form solution in the binary model.
The resulting rent follows from limited liability and noisy outcome information.
Monitoring
A signal may be observed without being contract-verifiable.
Contract triggers require applicable evidence, not merely private awareness.
Hold-up
Specific investment can be socially efficient and privately rejected.
Ex post bargaining can destroy ex ante investment incentives.
Bargaining
Under the symmetric Nash-product concept, excess transferable surplus is split equally above outside options.
Bargaining predictions remain solution-concept dependent.
Multiple equilibria and credibility
One desired Nash equilibrium does not establish full implementation.
Nash equilibrium can rely on a noncredible off-path threat.
Subgame perfection removes the specific threat in the entry-game construction.
Coalitions
Individual strategyproofness does not imply coalition-proofness.
Side payments can change coalition incentives.
Exhaustive coalition checking can require exponentially many coalitions.
Contract semantics
Contracts can be represented as typed state machines.
Formal contract satisfaction does not prove legal enforceability.
A contractual obligation does not imply physical performance.
Physical performance does not imply contractual authorization.
A valid versioned change order updates the formal contract baseline.
Responsibility
Causal contribution does not generically determine contractual responsibility.
Contractual responsibility does not itself determine legal liability.
Responsibility closure is sound relative to the declared dispute fiber.
Incomplete evidence can leave responsibility nonidentifiable.
The correct result under multiple surviving responsibility reports is unresolved.
Cross-layer integration
Architectural authority remains a hard constraint on mechanism outcomes.
Mechanism transfers must pass Prompt-18 cash-flow checking.
Strategic actions must respect Prompt-19 commitment windows.
Reliability reports remain Prompt-17 claims rather than strategic facts.
Fleet evidence remains Prompt-16 evidence rather than individual type truth.
Prompt-9 schedules investigations, while Prompt 20 governs incentive and contract structure.
Prompt-10 can produce strategic and contractual breakers.
Assurance and checking
Governance claims retain exact DSIC, BIC, Nash, SPNE, coalition, contract, evidence, or legal-unresolved modalities.
Incremental governance recertification equals cold checking under complete dependencies.
Explicit finite DSIC checking has a precise enumeration complexity.
PCGC checking is linear in proof-DAG size plus local checker costs.
Governance-certificate semantics are independent of proposal source.
Whole-project governance
A checked mechanism and strategy profile can strategically implement a target policy under the declared equilibrium mode.
Participation, equilibrium, coalition, contract, responsibility, and legal conclusions remain separately typed.
Prompt-11 assurance may consume only the governance propositions that the PCGC actually proves.



