70. Final Charge
A mathematically optimal contract is not automatically a legally enforceable contract.
A socially optimal project outcome is not automatically an equilibrium.
A Nash equilibrium is not automatically efficient.
A Bayesian equilibrium is not a dominant-strategy equilibrium.
A dominant-strategy mechanism is not automatically coalition proof.
An incentive-compatible report is not a verified physical fact.
A voluntary participant may reject a socially beneficial project when its own outside option is better.
A supplier that truthfully reports cost may still misrepresent capacity.
A low bid is not evidence of technical qualification.
A bonus creates effort incentives only when observable outcomes actually depend on effort.
An event someone personally observes is not automatically contract-verifiable.
A contractual obligation is not physical performance.
Physical performance is not automatically authorized work.
Causal responsibility is not contractual responsibility.
Contractual responsibility is not automatically legal liability.
One good equilibrium is not enough when another equilibrium produces failure.
A threat written into a strategy is not credible merely because it supports an earlier Nash equilibrium.
Individual strategyproofness does not eliminate collusion.
A change order is not merely another number in a budget.
It changes:
scope;
time;
cost;
risk;
rights;
obligations;
evidence;
versions;
sometimes architectural authority.
