The strategic-implementation law is:
[ \boxed{ \textbf{A centralized optimum becomes a project outcome only through a mechanism under which the participating agents possess admissible strategies that implement it.} } ]
The incentive law is:
[ \boxed{ \textbf{Truthful reporting is a theorem only relative to a mechanism, type space, utility model, information structure, and equilibrium concept.} } ]
The evidence law is:
[ \boxed{ \textbf{Making truth telling optimal does not make a reported statement independently verified.} } ]
The participation law is:
[ \boxed{ \textbf{A voluntary project arrangement must make participation acceptable to every required participant under the applicable participation criterion.} } ]
The procurement law is:
[ \boxed{ \textbf{Mechanism design may elicit price information, but technical qualification, capacity, evidence, reliability, and common-cause dependence remain separate engineering claims.} } ]
The adverse-selection law is:
[ \boxed{ \textbf{When one party knows something the project does not, contract design must reason about what that party has an incentive to reveal—not merely what ANANKE wishes it knew.} } ]
The moral-hazard law is:
[ \boxed{ \textbf{When effort is hidden, incentives can act only through signals whose distributions actually depend on that effort and whose contractual meaning is verifiable.} } ]
The hold-up law is:
[ \boxed{ \textbf{A future bargaining process can destroy the incentive to make a valuable irreversible investment today.} } ]
The bargaining law is:
[ \boxed{ \textbf{Bargaining mathematics characterizes outcomes only after the solution concept, outside options, and feasible surplus have been declared.} } ]
The equilibrium law is:
[ \boxed{ \textbf{The existence of one desirable equilibrium does not prove that the project will select it.} } ]
The credibility law is:
[ \boxed{ \textbf{A threat that would not be rational to execute when its node is reached cannot be treated as a credible project commitment merely because it supports an earlier Nash equilibrium.} } ]
The coalition law is:
[ \boxed{ \textbf{Individual incentive compatibility does not guarantee resistance to coordinated deviations, collusion, or hidden side payments.} } ]
The contract law is:
[ \boxed{ \textbf{A proof-carrying contract model establishes exactly the obligations, rights, and transitions of its declared formal semantics—not every consequence a real legal system may attach to the words.} } ]
The change-order law is:
[ \boxed{ \textbf{A project change alters the formal contractual baseline only through the authority, evidence, version, and amendment semantics required by the active governance theory.} } ]
The responsibility law is:
[ \boxed{ \textbf{ANANKE must keep separate who caused an event, who contractually bears its consequence, what the evidence proves, and what external law ultimately decides.} } ]
The dispute law is:
[ \boxed{ \textbf{When several evidence-consistent project histories assign responsibility differently, the mathematically correct result is unresolved responsibility—not fabricated certainty.} } ]
The sovereignty law is:
[ \boxed{ \textbf{No incentive mechanism acquires authority to trade away protected architecture, safety, or human authorship merely because doing so improves total modeled surplus.} } ]
The open-world law is:
[ \boxed{ \textbf{No strategic model proves alignment against preferences, private information, side agreements, irrationality, or legal doctrines that the model never represented.} } ]
The deepest Prompt-20 principle is:
[ \boxed{ \begin{minipage}{0.91\linewidth} ANANKE becomes a genuine multi-party project system only when it distinguishes the engineering question “what should happen?” from the institutional question “why would each party actually do it?” The objective is not to replace human negotiation with one synthetic utility function. It is to make every claim about incentives, participation, contracts, strategic behavior, responsibility, and dispute resolution carry the exact agent model, information structure, authority, evidence, equilibrium concept, and execution assumptions that make that claim true. \end{minipage} } ]
